4.The Formal Proof of Bias
Take expectation of the MLE estimator. The key decomposition is
\[\begin{align}
x_i -\bar{x} = (x_i - \mu^*)-(\bar{x}-\mu^*)
\end{align}\]
Substituting and expanding:
\[\begin{align}
\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i -\bar{x})(x_i-\bar{x})^T=\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\mu^*)(x_i-\mu^*)-n(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T
\end{align}\]
Taking expectations of both sides, and using the fact that \(Var(\bar{x})=\frac{\sum}{n}\)
\[\begin{align}
E\bigg[\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\bar{x})(x_i-\bar{x})^T\bigg] = nΣ -Σ = (n-1)Σ
\end{align}\]
So:
\[\begin{align}
E[\hat{\sum}_{MLE}]=\frac{1}{n}(n-1)\sum = \frac{n-1}{n}\sum
\end{align}\]
The MLE is always off by the factor \(\frac{n-1}{n}\) - always less than the truth. Dividing by \(n-1\) instead of \(n\) corrects this exactly.

Little more explanation of the above point
\[\begin{align}
x_i-\bar{x} = (x_i - \mu^*)-(\bar{x}-\mu^*)
\end{align}\]
\[\begin{align}
(x_i-\bar{x})(x_i-\bar{x})^T =(x_i-\mu^*)(x_i-\mu^*)^T-(x_i-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T-(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(x_i-\mu^*)^T + (\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T
\end{align}\]
Sum over all \(n\) Points
\[\begin{align}
\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\bar{x})(x_i-\bar{x})^T =\sum_{i=1}(x_i-\mu^*)(x_i-\mu^*)^T-\bigg(\sum_{i=1}(x_i-\mu^*)\bigg)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T-(\bar{x}-\mu^*)\bigg(\sum_{i=1}(x_i-\mu^*)^T\bigg) +n(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T
\end{align}\]
Focusing on the middle two terms
\[\begin{align}
\sum_{i=1}(x_i-\mu^*) = \sum_{i=1}^n x_i - n\mu^*=n\bar{x}-n\mu^*=n(\bar{x}-\mu^*)
\end{align}\]
So the second term becomes
\[\begin{align}
-\bigg(\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\mu^*)\bigg)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)=-n(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T
\end{align}\]
And the third term becomes
\[\begin{align}
-(\bar{x}-\mu^*)\bigg(\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\mu^*)\bigg)^T=-n(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T
\end{align}\]
Putting it all together
\[\begin{align}
\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\bar{x})(x_i-\bar{x})^T = \sum_{i=1}(x_i-\mu^*)(x_i-\mu^*)^T - \underbrace{n(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T-n(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T}_{-2n(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T} + n(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T
\end{align}\]
Combining the last three terms.
\[\begin{align}
\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\bar{x})(x_i-\bar{x})^T = \sum_{i=1}(x_i-\mu^*)(x_i-\mu^*)^T -n(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T
\end{align}\]
Take the Expectations of Each term
Now we apply \(\mathbb{E}[\cdot]\) to both sides.
\[\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}\bigg[\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\mu^*)(x_i-\mu^*)^T\bigg]=\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{E}[(x_i-\mu^*)(x_i-\mu^*)^T]
\end{align}\]
Each term in the sum is just the definition of \(\sum\) - the covariance of a single observation
\[\begin{align}
\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{E}[(x_i-\mu^*)(x_i-\mu^*)^T] = Σ
\end{align}\]
Example : Let \(x_i \in \mathbb{R}^2\) , with true mean \(\mu^* =\big(\frac{1}{2}\big)\) and covariance
\[\begin{align}
Σ=\begin{pmatrix}
4  & 1\
1 & 3
\end{pmatrix}
\end{align}\]x} \end{align}
The deviation from the mean is the random vector:
\[\begin{align}
x_i-\mu^* = \begin{pmatrix}
x_{i1}-1\
x_{i2}-2
\end{pmatrix}
\end{align}\]x} \end{align}
The outer product is
\[\begin{align}
(x_i-\mu^*)(x_i-\mu^*)^T = \begin{pmatrix}
(x_{i1}-1)^2 & (x_{i1}-1)(x_{i2}-2))\
(x_{i2}-2)(x_{i1}-1) & (x_{i2}-2)^2 )
\end{pmatrix}
\end{align}\]x} \end{align}
So summing over \(n\) points: = \(nΣ\)
Second term on right - expectation of the correction term:
\[\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}[n(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T]=n\cdot\mathbb{E}[(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T]
\end{align}\]
The inner expectation is jus tthe covariance of \(\bar{x}\) itself.
\[\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}[(\bar{x}-\mu^*)(\bar{x}-\mu^*)^T]= Var(\bar{x})
\end{align}\]
Since ${x}=$ and the \(x_i\) are i.i.d with covariance \(Σ\)
\[\begin{align}
Var(\bar{x}) = \frac{1}{n^2}\cdot nΣ = \frac{Σ}{n}
\end{align}\]
So the second term becomes:
\[\begin{align}
n \cdot \frac{Σ}{n} = Σ
\end{align}\]
Combining
\[\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}\bigg[\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\bar{x})(x_i-\bar{x})^T\bigg] = nΣ-Σ=(n-1)Σ
\end{align}\]
Therefore, the expectation of the MLE estimator is:
\[\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}[\hat{\sum}_{MLE}]=\frac{1}{n} \mathbb{E}\bigg[\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i=\bar{x})(x_i-\bar{x})^T\bigg] = \frac{(n-1)}{n}Σ
\end{align}\]
The MLE is always off by the factor \(\frac{n-1}{n}\) - always less than the truth. Dividing my \(n-1\) instead of \(n\) corrects this exactly.
